\subsection{Peak-period to zero-crossing-period bridge}
\label{sec:brmm-tp-tz-bridge}

For every COMPLETE-BRMM statement in this section, physical admission includes
Section~\ref{sec:brmm-physical-wave-generator}, its fixed hard family budgets,
and the same generator potential across successive words. Finite-window
compactness alone is not a replacement for that condition.


When a spectral model is used to interpret a BRMM history, its components may
be parameterized by peak periods $T_{p,r}$, but the deployed tuner does \emph{not} estimate a peak
period.  Its measurement-derived canonical period state is the zero-crossing/
moment period $T_z$ produced by \texttt{WavePeriodEstimator}.  These quantities
must therefore remain distinct. The spectral bridge below is optional;
BRMM admission itself requires no spectral decomposition or fixed number of
sea partitions.

For a scalar spectrum $\Phi(\omega)$ with angular-frequency moments
\begin{equation}
 m_j[\Phi]:=\int_0^\infty \omega^j\Phi(\omega)\,d\omega,
 \label{eq:brmm-spectral-moments}
\end{equation}
the corresponding ideal zero-crossing period is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{T_z[\Phi]=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m_0[\Phi]}{m_2[\Phi]}}.}
 \label{eq:brmm-tz-moment}
\end{equation}
For a multimodal sea the spectral moments add before this ratio is formed;
periods are not averaged independently.  If the normalized partition energies
are
\begin{equation}
 w_r=\frac{H_r^2}{\sum_j H_j^2},
\end{equation}
then the zero-crossing periods of uncorrelated component elevation spectra obey
the exact mixture identity
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \frac{1}{T_{z,\mathrm{mix}}^2}
 =\sum_r\frac{w_r}{T_{z,r}^2}.
 }
 \label{eq:brmm-multimodal-tz}
\end{equation}
Thus $T_{z,\mathrm{mix}}$ lies between the active component extrema, but it is
not an arithmetic average of their periods.  Consequently two partitions with
the same $T_p$ but different $\gamma$, energy share, direction, or vessel
response need not induce the same tuner period.

The replay-free spectral diagnostic,
\texttt{ou3\_brmm\_spectral\_moment\_bridge.json}, isolates the frequency-shape
part of this bridge before any vessel response is applied.  On its provisional
JONSWAP shape screen $1\le\gamma\le7$ with the standard piecewise peak widths
$0.07/0.09$, the PM boundary $\gamma=1$ is analytical:
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \left.\frac{T_{z,\eta}}{T_p}\right|_{\gamma=1}
 =\left(\frac54\pi\right)^{-1/4}
 =0.710370680986\ldots .
 }
 \label{eq:brmm-pm-tz-tp}
\end{equation}
A 240-cell floating-point continuum screen, with conservative quadrature and
analytical tail pads, currently encloses the \emph{surface-elevation} ratio as
\begin{equation}
 0.70911959\lesssim\frac{T_{z,\eta}}{T_p}\lesssim0.82908369.
 \label{eq:brmm-jonswap-tz-tp-screen}
\end{equation}
This numerical interval is deliberately non-promoting: its quadrature has not
yet been replaced by validated interval integration.  The exact PM identity and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-multimodal-tz} are analytical; the continuum endpoints are
only a feasibility screen for a validated spectral enclosure.

For a spectral family whose BRMM premises have separately been established,
any tighter tuner bound must certify a relation
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 (\lambda,x^s)\in\widehat{\mathcal S}_{\rm spec}
 \Longrightarrow
 T_z^{\rm src}\in\mathcal T_z(\lambda,x^s),
 }
 \label{eq:brmm-tp-tz-relation}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda$ collects spectral/response parameters and $x^s$ retains
their common physical history. For a directional elevation spectrum
$E(\omega,\vartheta;\lambda)$ and sea-to-IMU response $G_{\rm imu}$, the
corresponding matrix spectrum is
\begin{equation}
 \Phi_u(\omega;\lambda)=\int
 G_{\rm imu}E(\omega,\vartheta;\lambda)G_{\rm imu}^{*}\,d\vartheta.
 \label{eq:brmm-response-spectrum}
\end{equation}
The response and cross spectra must belong to that same physical history.
For an analytical spectral enclosure, Eq.~\eqref{eq:brmm-tz-moment} supplies
the moment bridge; for the final source certificate the leaky filters, finite
moment EMAs, log-period state, initialization, and clamps are propagated
explicitly.  Thus neither the surface quantity $T_{z,\eta}$ nor a bound on
$T_{p,r}$ alone is substituted for a bound on the shipping tuner state.

The period-source path also has a startup branch that is part of the theorem
language rather than a nuisance transient to be discarded.  Shipping code
supplies the fixed $0.2$ Hz prior until the same WavePeriodEstimator state
satisfies \texttt{hasUsablePeriod()}: moments start after $3/\lambda$, startup
use is forbidden before $4/\lambda$, and at least one current estimated period
of moment history is required.  The stricter \texttt{isReady()} diagnostic
retains its $6/\lambda$ floor and debiased-weight test.  The tuner consumes the
period source before the current sample advances the WavePeriodEstimator, so
the first newly usable estimator frequency can affect the tuner no earlier
than the following valid sample. Therefore the source-to-tuner bridge must
enclose both the prior-driven and startup-usable estimator-driven branches and
their one-sample takeover edge.

The same qualification is essential for acceleration variance.  The ideal
JONSWAP high-frequency elevation tail is proportional to $\omega^{-5}$, whereas
specific acceleration multiplies elevation power by $\omega^4$.  An unbounded
idealized acceleration second moment would therefore contain an asymptotic
$\int d\omega/\omega$ term and is not a finite certificate quantity.  The
heuristic scaling $\sigma_{a}T_p^2/H_s=O(1)$ becomes usable only after the
certified vessel/IMU response and the deployed finite wave band are included.
Source qualification must bound that response-weighted, band-limited statistic;
it must not use
an unbanded JONSWAP acceleration variance as a theorem assumption.

The current $0.03$--$1.2$ Hz range is consequently a bound on the
\emph{committed tuning-frequency channel}, not an asserted $T_p$ range for the
physical sea.  The BRMM P2 refinement may use a tighter $T_z/T_p$ relation only
after it has been certified over the declared JONSWAP/PM mixture and directional
response family and propagated it through the exact prior/estimator source
state. Such a spectral tightening requires both the response and estimator
bridge; a history qualified directly under BRMM may instead establish tuner
reachability from its physical and execution constraints.
